Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, the outpatient therapy **treatment plan (plan of care)** must include all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any **related medical diagnoses**;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved within the duration of the plan**;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the start date** of the plan.  
  [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) § 3.2.1.6 (effective Feb. 1, 2025; current per amendment check)

## Documentation requirement
In addition to containing those plan elements, the provider’s records must include **a copy of the treatment plan** in the beneficiary’s documentation. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) § 7.2 (effective Feb. 1, 2025; current per amendment check)

## Practical takeaway
So, for outpatient therapy under Policy 10A, the plan of care is not just a general note that therapy is needed. It must be a **signed, time-limited, discipline-specific treatment plan** with measurable goals, service frequency, visit length, and the interventions to be provided.

Physicians should provide Medicaid-covered fluoride varnish **from tooth eruption until the child reaches 3½ years of age**. The service is **limited to beneficiaries under 3½ years of age**, may be provided **once every 60 calendar days**, and is **limited to six times before the beneficiary reaches 3½**. [Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment) (effective Dec. 4, 2019), §§ 7.2, 5.3.1, 5.3.2. I checked amendments for this policy document and the corpus identifies Policy 1A-23 itself as the authoritative current document, with no listed amendments.

## Oral screening requirements

Under NC Medicaid’s **Physician Fluoride Varnish Services** policy, the oral screening must include:

- **Early caries screening**;
- **Detection of notable findings** in the mouth, including obvious hard- and soft-tissue pathology, using **a dental mirror and directed light**;
- **Counseling and educational materials** on good oral hygiene and nutrition for children;
- **Prescribing a fluoride supplement, if indicated**; and
- **Application of fluoride varnish to all erupted primary teeth** beginning at tooth eruption until age 3½. [Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment), § 7.2.

## Required documentation

The beneficiary’s health record must document all of the following:

- the **oral evaluation** and any notable findings;
- **preventive oral health and dietary counseling** with the primary caregiver;
- **application of fluoride varnish**; and
- **referral to a dentist, if appropriate**. [Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment), § 7.2.

## Billing-related screening rule

The oral evaluation code **D0145**:
- is for a patient under three years of age with counseling of the primary caregiver;
- includes early caries screening, caries-risk evaluation, and recording other notable oral findings;
- **must be billed in conjunction with D1206**; and
- is subject to the same **under-3½ / every-60-days / six-visit** limits. [Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment), §§ 5.3.1, 5.3.2.

