Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must be certified by meeting the provider standards in Policy 12B, and it can be decertified for failing those standards or for specified compliance violations.

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid Policy 12B says an HIV case management provider must:

- have a **documented successful record of 3 years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are delivered by **qualified case managers**;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors**;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV case management provider;
- meet **applicable state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records** and inspect agency operations and financial records;
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed business changes within **30 calendar days of the proposed change** and no later than **5 business days after the actual change**; and
- achieve **national accreditation within one year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the designated accrediting bodies: ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

The recertification checklist reinforces that providers must submit information showing, among other things:

- all case managers and supervisors with dates of employment;
- all counties where HIV case management is provided;
- services offered;
- operating hours and an emergency after-hours plan;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- satisfaction survey frequency and how results are used; and
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf)

Policy 12B also includes application/organizational requirements such as a **physical business site** verified by site visit, organizational chart, ownership information, access to required web-based applications, a business plan, adequate computer/HIPAA/electronic billing capability, compliance with precertification onsite review, and written organizational policies including confidentiality, grievance, rights, nondiscrimination, ethics, conflict of interest, records, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation policies. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## Grounds for decertification

Policy 12B states a provider may be decertified for any of the following:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing**;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** within 30 calendar days for unmet requirements cited by NC Medicaid or its designated contractor;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet policy qualifications;
- staff failure to complete required **basic training within 90 days** of employment;
- staff failure to obtain required **continuing education units**;
- failure to provide staff supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within required time frames;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid of agency changes so that the agency cannot be contacted;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months of certification**; or
- failure to achieve or maintain the **certification requirements in subsection 6.2**. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## Practical takeaway

In practice, certification depends on three main categories:

1. **Organizational capacity** — experience, site enrollment, business infrastructure, written policies, and accreditation.
2. **Qualified staffing and supervision** — proper case manager/supervisor qualifications, training, and CEUs.
3. **Ongoing compliance** — documentation, quality improvement, cooperation with oversight, and timely notice of operational changes.

A provider can be decertified either for serious misconduct like fraud or falsified records, or for compliance failures such as inadequate staffing qualifications, missing training/CEUs, deficient documentation, failure to correct cited deficiencies, or loss of certification prerequisites.

